%% Answer plans for every past T4 question — RN volume only.

## Every past question as an answer plan

Use these plans in the last fortnight: read the question, say the plan aloud from memory, then check. Each plan is the skeleton of the full model answer in T4QA.

**2016 Q8 — Critically review WID, WAD and GAD.** Include → question → transform. Welfare before 1970; Boserup 1970. WID: Washington, Percy 1973, UN Decade; integration, access, liberal feminism; contributions (visibility, ministries, resources); limits (add women and stir; division of labour taken for granted; women alone; class blind; small projects; efficiency). WAD: Marxism, dependency, IDS 1978; always integrated, unequally; contributions ("for whom?", class, debt); limits (patriarchy within classes; household; women's projects). GAD: socialist feminism, DAWN 1984, Beijing 1995; gender relations, production and reproduction, agency, transformation; tools (PGN/SGN — Molyneux 1985, Moser 1993; triple role; Kabeer 1999); limits (checklists; hides women; mainstreamed away; resistance; "donor language"). Pyramid; table. Pakistan: Women's Division 1979, First Women Bank 1989 (WID); mainstreaming, BISP, 2020 property law (GAD); 143/145; 24.4%.

**2017 Q6 — Globalisation has highlighted gender issues; examples from developing countries.** Define (Giddens). Dimensions; WTO 1995. Visibility: norms (Beijing, CEDAW, SDGs), media (#MeToo), supply-chain scrutiny (Clean Clothes; Bangladesh Accord 2013), migration. Opportunities: Bangladesh garments (Kabeer 2000), Malaysia/Philippines electronics, Indian call centres, Kenyan mobile money. Harms: feminisation (Standing 1989), maquiladoras and EPZs, home-based subcontracting (India, Pakistan, Thailand), care chains (Philippines, Sri Lanka; *kafala*; Hochschild 2000), Rana Plaza 2013, Ali Enterprises 2012, trafficking (Nepal), factory harassment. Culture: GSP+, skin-lightening, backlash. Compare figure. Pakistan (textiles, HBW Act 2018, remittances 38bn+, GSP+ 2014, 24.4%, Sialkot and the Atlanta Agreement 1997). Policy decides.

**2017 Q8(b) — Short note: gender and development.** (~555 words.) Field vs GAD approach. Beyond WID and WAD (neither examined power or the household). Origins: Kate Young, IDS, DAWN 1984, Beijing 1995. Seven principles incl. active state. Tools flow figure (PGN/SGN, triple role, empowerment, mainstreaming). Strengths; limits (checklists, hides women, resistance). Pakistan (budgeting, 10% quota, BISP, 2020 law; 24.4%). Asks whether power changes.

**2018 Q6 — Global political economy and Pakistani women.** Define GPE (states, markets, IMF, WTO, UN, MNCs). Periphery/lower semi-periphery; 144th economic rank. Channels: textiles (garment jobs; home-based piece work; housewifisation; Sindh 2018); supply chains (Ali Enterprises; Atlanta 1997; GSP+ 2014); IMF (1988 onwards; 2024 EFF; prices, sales tax, cuts; Elson; BISP expanded); migration (38bn+; women heading households; women migrants and care chains); agriculture (cotton pickers; 2022 food prices and floods); digital (freelancing; 25% gap; platform insecurity); norms (Beijing, CEDAW, SDG 5 → NCSW, seats, laws; donor-driven); culture (beauty, backlash). Mapping figure. Policy. National policy decides.

**2018 Q8(a) — Short note: strategic and practical gender needs.** (~555 words.) Molyneux 1985 (Nicaragua; interests) → Moser 1989/1993 (needs; GAD). PGNs: immediate, material, from current roles; water, health, food, fuel, housing, childcare, income; short term; do not challenge; MCQ 2026. SGNs: from analysis of subordination; land, inheritance, laws against violence, equal wages, marriage choice, body, representation; long term; resisted. Table (nature, roles, examples, who defines, Moser's approaches, Pakistan). Simple box (well vs control of the well). Link: meeting PGNs via women's organisations opens SGNs; water project with women's committee. Pakistan: LHWs (1994; SC 2012), BISP, 2020 property law, ICT 2025.

**2019 Q4 — Modernisation and dependency; subordination in modern societies.** Two rival theories. Modernisation (Rostow 1960; five stages; tradition vs Western values; trickle-down); relevance (education, cities, media; real cultural obstacles); critique (Boserup; culture blamed; West not equal). Dependency (Prebisch, Frank 1966, Rodney; centre/periphery; produced underdevelopment); relevance (cheap female labour; debt and services; structures); critique (gender-blind; male industrial hero; local patriarchy; unpaid work — Mies). Compare figure. Modern societies: double burden, glass ceiling, pay gap, beauty industry (structures not only attitudes); global chains, Rana Plaza, austerity. What both miss: household bargaining (Sen 1990; Agarwal), unpaid care, agency. Pakistan: enrolment and fertility vs textiles and IMF; 24.4%; 143/145. Join cultural and structural.

**2019 Q5 — Gendered inequalities created by globalisation.** Accept premise; define; global village (WTO 1995, chains, phones). Inequalities: between nations (Wallerstein; brands shift orders; 69.2%, 120 years); feminised flexible informal labour (Standing; EPZs; housewifisation — Mies); wage and occupational gaps (part-time, home-based); care chains (Hochschild; *kafala*; grandmothers left caring); unsafe work (Rana Plaza, Ali Enterprises; harassment); austerity (Elson; girls withdrawn); trafficking, commodification (surrogacy, sex industry, skin-lightening); digital divide (25%) and backlash. Cycle figure. Counter-evidence (Kabeer 2000; Bangladesh Accord 2013). Pakistan (home-based, migration, IMF, 24.4%, Sindh 2018). Gendered terms; policy.

**2019 Q8(c) — Short note: structural functionalism.** (~550 words.) System of parts; stability (MCQ); living body; equilibrium. Durkheim; Parsons (pattern variables); Merton; Murdock 1949 (four functions). Parsons and Bales 1955 — instrumental/expressive (compare figure); functions: socialisation and stabilisation. Link to development (extension to men; cooking classes to women). Critique: justifies inequality; power and conflict; 1950s white middle-class (working-class and Black women always worked); hides women's work; cannot explain change (married women's paid work). Friedan 1963; Oakley 1974. Pakistan: provider–homemaker ideal vs rural work, two incomes, migration. Useful: institutions, socialisation, dysfunction.

**2020 Q3 — How the colonial era influenced women's status in the subcontinent.** Rearranged patriarchy. Gaze (Mill 1817; Mayo 1927; Gandhi; Spivak; Mani). Reform laws table (1829, 1856, 1870, 1891, 1929, 1939; from Indian reformers; weak enforcement). Codified personal law (Anglo-Muhammadan law; Punjab Laws Act 1872 → lost inheritance). Land settlements in men's names. Economy (machine cloth; cash crops; unpaid work invisible). Bodies (Contagious Diseases Acts; Criminal Tribes Act 1871; IPC 1860 s. 497). Education (Aligarh 1906; elite). Mapping figure. Nationalist response (Chatterjee 1989; Nazir Ahmad; Mumtaz Ali; symbols). Assessment (gains vs losses; officials reluctant to interfere). Legacy (PPC; Sarda Act until Sindh 2013/ICT 2025; land and inheritance; *khawaja siras*; symbols).

**2021 Q6 — Modernisation blames internal cultural factors.** Accurate but incomplete. Theory (Rostow 1960, Cold War; Parsons, Lerner 1958, Inkeles and Smith 1974). Tradition vs modernity. Elaborate: seclusion and honour; early marriage and fertility; son preference; religion (texts vs customs); rural "backwardness". Flow figure. Cure: education, media, cities, family planning, technology; women as mothers; trickle-down. Where it holds (child marriage, honour killing, inheritance; education works). Critiques: Boserup (male/female farming systems); structures ignored (dependency, world-system); West as false model (Friedan; reform looks foreign); "tradition" colonial and changing (Punjab 1872). Compare figure. Pakistan (Ayub, Green Revolution, MFLO 1961; today 24.4% and structural barriers). Culture one cause among many; change laws, property, markets too.

**2021 Q7 — Approaches to women's development: WID, WAD, GAD.** Near repeat — re-cut around "approaches". Welfare (Moser; mothers; invisible producers). WID (Boserup, Washington, Percy); three variants (equity, anti-poverty, efficiency); strengths; weaknesses (efficiency used unpaid time in adjustment). WAD (Marxism, dependency, IDS 1978; Southern voice); strengths; weaknesses (NIEO hard to adopt). GAD (Kate Young, DAWN 1984, Beijing); principles; tools; strengths (collective organisation); weaknesses. Spectrum figure (welfare → GAD empowerment). Practical–strategic test. Pakistan (welfare, WID institutions; GAD tools; 24.4%; 143/145). From welfare to empowerment; implementation and political will.

**2022 Q7 — Capitalistic perspectives of gender.** Define capitalism. Liberal market view (skills not sex; barriers; Kabeer 2000; smart economics WDR 2012 vs Chant and Sweetman 2012). Critical view: Engels 1884; separate spheres and breadwinner (Hartmann 1979 family wage); unpaid labour/social reproduction (Benston 1969; Dalla Costa and James 1972; Federici 1975; Waring 1988); reserve army (wartime) and feminisation (Standing 1989); housewifisation (Mies 1986, Narsapur); Federici 2004 witch-hunts. Compare figure. Global capitalism (EPZs, chains, care chains; Rana Plaza; Ali Enterprises). Pakistan (home-based; Sindh 2018; cotton; unpaid care; 24.4% — mobility, transport, harassment, childcare). Access to paid work plus the 3 Rs of care, with labour rights.

**2022 Q8 — Gender critique of SAPs.** Define SAPs; Mexico 1982; Williamson 1989 (goals set in Washington). Conditions table. Gender-blind: invisible care economy; Elson 1991 "infinitely elastic". Unpaid work rises (sick at home; stretching food; time-use surveys). Health (fees; maternal care; women delay own treatment) and girls' schooling. Jobs (public cuts; informal work invisible). Export jobs (cheap labour for foreign exchange; insecurity). Flow figure. Food prices, stress, violence; breadwinner loss. No voice (budgets judged by deficits). Evidence (UNICEF 1987; Commonwealth 1989; DAWN 1985); response (safety nets; PRSPs 1999; recipients not citizens). Pakistan (1988; SBA 2023; EFF 2024; prices, sales tax, cuts; BISP expanded). Gender-assessed stabilisation; human face.

**2023 Q6 — Detailed note on colonial and capitalistic perspectives.** Both reshaped by power and profit. Colonial: gaze (Mill, Mayo, Spivak, Mani), policies (reform laws; codified law, Punjab 1872; land and machine cloth; Contagious Diseases Acts, Criminal Tribes Act), nationalist response (Chatterjee; Nazir Ahmad, Mumtaz Ali; reform as protecting "our" women). Capitalist: market view (smart economics; critics) and critical view (Engels; separate spheres; social reproduction; reserve army and feminisation). Housewifisation (Mies — joins both). Venn figure. Four links (colonialism as capitalist expansion; public/private split; extraction of women's work; civilisation → development). Pakistan (PPC 1860; inheritance and land; home-based work, Sindh 2018; Ali Enterprises; 24.4% in agriculture and informal work).

**2024 Q3 — Colonial and capitalistic perspectives; development as the new coloniality.** Coloniality (Quijano 2000). Compact note on both perspectives; Mies's link. Lugones 2007 (coloniality of gender). Development as discourse (Truman 1949; Escobar 1995; Sachs 1992). Thesis applied: "Third World woman" (Mohanty 1984; Abu-Lughod 2002); experts, donors, beneficiaries, debt (Elson); supply-chain extraction (Rana Plaza, Ali Enterprises); imposed gender models and indicators. Mapping figure. Counter-argument (real gains; Haq 1990; DAWN 1984; women's own demands; anti-colonial language used against women's rights). Pakistan (IMF 2024, donor projects, textile chains vs MFLO 1961, 2006, 2016, 2025 won by Pakistani women). Decolonise, don't abandon.

**2024 Q5 — Critical gender analysis of development theories.** Method: cause, women, assumptions, silences, policies. Modernisation (Rostow; mothers; trickle-down; Boserup; West as model). Dependency (Prebisch, Frank, Rodney; structures, debt; gender-blind; local patriarchy; Mies). World-system (Wallerstein; Smith and Wallerstein 1992; assembly line 1983; women central to capitalism but portrayed as victims; Kabeer 2000). Functionalism (Parsons and Bales 1955; justifies inequality; extension services; socialisation useful). Table. Shared blind spots (gender blindness; Waring 1988; Sen 1990 and Agarwal; passive women; male-centred progress). Responses: WID, WAD, GAD (Kabeer 1999). Later: Sen and Nussbaum 2000; Escobar, Mohanty; feminist economics; ecofeminism (Agarwal 1992). Pakistan: the cotton picker through each lens (24.4%). Gender analysis completes the theories.

**2025 Q3 — Gendered globalisation, world-system theory, new patriarchal power.** Define (uses, reshapes, creates; Acker, Sassen). Wallerstein 1974 (core, semi-periphery, periphery; unequal exchange). Blind spot → extensions (Smith and Wallerstein 1992; Mies 1986; Fuentes and Ehrenreich 1983; Ward 1990). Pyramid. New patriarchal power: workplace (targets, surveillance, harassment; young women preferred as less likely to organise); feminised informal labour (Standing; home as workplace); care chains (Hochschild; *kafala*); debt and austerity (Elson); commodified bodies and backlash. Evidence of inequality (Rana Plaza; Ali Enterprises; 69.2%, 120 years). Counter-evidence (Kabeer 2000; CEDAW, SDG 5; Bangladesh Accord 2013; agency). Compare figure. Pakistan (periphery; home-based; Sindh 2018; remittances; GSP+ 2014; 24.4%). Largely true but incomplete.

**2025 Q7 — WID and GAD: principles and limitations.** Include vs transform. WID origins (Boserup; Washington; Percy 1973; Decade); principles (five); limitations (division of labour; practical not strategic; women alone; small projects in crowded "feminine" markets; class blind); strengths. GAD origins (Kate Young, IDS; DAWN 1984; Beijing 1995); principles (six); tools (Molyneux 1985, Moser 1993; triple role; Kabeer 1999; mainstreaming). Compare figure. GAD limitations (checklists; hides women; mainstreamed away; depoliticisation; resistance; urgent needs neglected; "donor language"). Compared (share vs power; many "GAD" programmes still WID in practice). Pakistan (WID institutions; GAD tools; 24.4%). Lessons (practical needs through women's organisations → strategic; LHWs). GAD analysis with WID delivery.

**2026 Q5 — Climate change: floods and heat waves in Pakistan; ecofeminism and feminist environmentalism.** Why gendered (dependence on water, fuel, fodder, livestock; little land and savings; mobility; care; voice). Ecofeminism (d'Eaubonne 1974; Shiva 1988; Chipko 1973). Feminist environmentalism (Agarwal 1992: material). Compare figure. Floods 2022 (33m; 1,700+ dead; a third under water; ~30bn dollars; 650,000 pregnant women). Impacts: maternal health; camp safety and privacy; menstrual hygiene; lost livestock and home-based work; relief to male heads and CNIC barriers; disease and care; girls out of school; early marriage reports. Heat (Jacobabad; kitchens; fields; pregnancy; water; dress and mobility; home-based income). Critical analysis (ecofeminism: extractive development and climate justice — Pakistan < 1% of emissions; essentialist; feminist environmentalism explains which women; intersectionality). Cycle figure. Frameworks (area K; Lima 2014; GAP 2017; NCCP 2021; ccGAP 2022; NDMA Gender and Child Cell; loss and damage COP27 2022). Policy list. Women at the centre of climate policy.
